      "text": "To find out how many students did not receive an A in either of the two courses, we start by using the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\nGiven:\n- Total number of students = 30\n- Number of students who received an A in history (H) = 7\n- Number of students who received an A in math (M) = 13\n- Number of students who received an A in both history and math (H ∩ M) = 4\n\nWe need to find the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses. \n\nStep 1: Calculate the number of students who received an A in both courses (H ∩ M).\nThis is given as 4.\n\nStep 2: Use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find the number of students who received an A in either history or math or both.\nTotal students in H ∪ M = Students in H + Students in M - (H ∩ M)\n= 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\nSo, 16 students received an A in either history or math or both.\n\nStep 3: Calculate the number of students who received an A in neither course.\nSince the total number of students is 30, we can find the number of students who did not receive an A in either course by subtracting the number of students who received an A in either history or math from the total number of students.\nNumber of students with an A in neither course = Total students - Students with an A in either course\n= 30 - 16 = 14\n\nThus, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\boxed{14}.",
